entries.body:
A discriminated union the size of typescript-go's node data now has a test, and the type table no longer does quadratic work on it. scripts/gen-union-scale.mjs writes tests/cases/union_scale.ts. That program has:
- an `enum Kind` of 200 members;
- 200 classes, each with its tag as the first field and 2-6 fields of numbers, strings, `Node | null` and `Node[]`;
- `type Node` over all of them;
- a 200-case `switch` that reads variant fields;
- a recursive visitor over a tree of 4,000 nodes.

It type-checks under `tsc --strict` and prints the same checksum natively and under Node. The switch lowers to an LLVM `switch`, a jump table at -O2, with only the tag load before it. tests/run.js checks that the case still matches its generator byte for byte.

In src/types.ts, `hasVariant` is now a binary search over a union's sorted variants. A union's display name and mangled name are now built by splitting the variant range in half, not by peeling one variant at a time. The names produced are unchanged, so no symbol, LLVM type name or golden moves.

Compile time still grows super-linearly past about 400 variants. The rest of the cost is in passes outside this change: `FactCollector`/`declarationIn` in src/attributes.ts (about half), `addMetadata` in src/ir.ts, and `checkOperator`/`narrowToTags` in src/expressions.ts.
